        "content": "# Module 19 - Complete Notes students.sort( Comparator.comparingInt( (Student s) -> s.marks ).reversed() ); Output: [Prasanna - 90, Anjali - 85, Rahul - 75] Module 22 — Generics in Java\n\nModule Flow GENERICS ↓ Why Generics? ↓ Type Safety ↓ Generic Classes ↓ Generic Methods ↓ Multiple Type Parameters ↓ Bounded Generics ↓ Wildcards (?, extends) ↓ Collections + Generics ↓ Practice ## What are Generics? Generics allow us to write classes, interfaces, and methods that work with different types while providing compile-time type safety .\nExample without generics:\nArrayList list = new ArrayList(); list.add(\"Prasanna\"); list.add(100); Different types can accidentally be stored. With generics: ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>(); Now the list is intended to contain only String objects. Generic ↓ Specify Type ↓ Compile-time checking ↓ Safer code ## Why Generics? ⭐⭐⭐ Consider: ArrayList list = new ArrayList(); list.add(\"Hello\"); String name = (String) list.get(0); Without generics, explicit casting may be required. With generics: ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>(); list.add(\"Hello\"); String name = list.get(0); No explicit cast is needed. Main benefits Generics ↓ Type Safety ↓ Less Casting ↓ Reusable Code ↓ Better Compile-time Checking ## Generic Collection Example ⭐⭐⭐ We already used: ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>(); Here: ArrayList ↓ Generic class ↓ <String> ↓ Type argument Similarly: ArrayList<Integer> marks = new ArrayList<>(); ArrayList<Double> prices = new ArrayList<>();\nExample ArrayList<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>(); numbers.add(10); numbers.add(20); numbers.add(30); This is valid. But:\n// numbers.add(\"Hello\"); //\n- causes a compile-time error.\n## Generic Class ⭐⭐⭐ We can create our own generic class. Syntax:\n```java\nclass Box<T> {\n    T value;\n```\nvoid set(T value) { this.value = value; } T get() { return value; } } Here: T ↓ Type parameter Use it: Box<String> box1 = new Box<>(); box1.set(\"Hello\");\n\n```java\nSystem.out.println(box1.get());\nOutput:\n```\nHello Another type: Box<Integer> box2 = new Box<>(); box2.set(100);\n\n```java\nSystem.out.println(box2.get());\nOutput:\n```\n100 ## Generic Class Flow Box<T> ↓ Type Parameter ↓\n```text\n┌──────────┴──────────┐\n``` ↓ ↓\nBox<String> Box<Integer> ↓ ↓ String Integer ↓ ↓ \"Hello\" 100 The same class can work with different types. Complete example\n```java\nclass Box<T> {\n    private T value;\n```\nBox(T value) { this.value = value; } T getValue() { return value; } } Use: Box<String> b1 = new Box<>(\"Java\"); Box<Integer> b2 = new Box<>(100); ## Generic Method ⭐⭐⭐ A method can also have its own type parameter. Example:\n```\n\nclass Demo {\n```\npublic static <T> void display(T value) {\n\n```java\nSystem.out.println(value);\n```\n} } Use: Demo.display(\"Hello\"); Demo.display(100); Demo.display(10.5); Output: Hello 100\n\n## 10.5 Syntax <T> void display(T value) Here: <T> ↓ Type parameter T value ↓ Parameter of that type ## Multiple Type Parameters A generic class can have more than one type parameter. 📝 **Example**\n>\n```java\nclass Pair<K, V> {\n    K key;\n```\nV value; Pair(K key, V value) { this.key = key; this.value = value; } void display() {\n\n```java\nSystem.out.println( key + \" : \" + value );\n```\n} } Use: Pair<Integer, String> student = new Pair<>(101, \"Prasanna\"); student.display(); Output: 101 : Prasanna Flow: Pair<K,V> ↓\n```text\n┌───────────────┐\n``` │ K → Key │ │ V → Value │ ```text\n└───────────────┘\n``` This idea is closely related to:\nHashMap<Integer, String> ## Bounded Generics ⭐⭐⭐ Sometimes we don't want to allow every type. We can restrict a type using: extends Example:\n```java\nclass NumberBox<T extends Number> {\n    T value;\n```\nNumberBox(T value) { this.value = value; } double getDoubleValue() { return value.doubleValue(); } } Now: NumberBox<Integer> b1 = new NumberBox<>(100); NumberBox<Double> b2 = new NumberBox<>(10.5); Both work because: Integer ↓ Number Double ↓ Number But: // NumberBox<String> b = // new NumberBox<>(\"Hello\"); //\n- because String is not a subclass of Number.\n## Wildcards ⭐⭐⭐ A wildcard is represented by: ? Example: List<?> list; It means a list of some type , but the exact type is unknown. 📝 **Example**\n>\npublic static void display( List<?> list) { for (Object item : list) {\n```java\nSystem.out.println(item);\n```\n} } This method can accept: List<String> List<Integer> List<Double> ? extends List<? extends Number> means a list whose element type is Number or a subtype of Number.\nExamples:\nList<Integer> ✅ List<Double> ✅ List<Float> ✅ But: List<String>\n- ## Complete Example + Revision\n```\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\n```\n```java\nclass Box<T> {\n    private T value;\n```\nBox(T value) { this.value = value; } public T getValue() { return value; } }\n```\n\nclass Main {\n```\npublic static <T> void display(T value) {\n\n```java\nSystem.out.println(value);\n```\n}\n\n```java\npublic static void main(String[] args) {\n    Box<String> name = new Box<>(\"Prasanna\");\n```\nBox<Integer> marks = new Box<>(90); System.out.println( name.getValue() ); System.out.println( marks.getValue() ); display(\"Java\"); display(100); } } Output: Prasanna 90",
